menu "Recovery (boot guard, gadget watchdog)"

    config MUSE_BOOTGUARD
        bool "Boot guard: escape a crash loop by itself"
        default y if MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE || MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO
        default n
        help
            The boot guard (muse_bootguard.h): three boots in a row
            that never get healthy (BLE up and 30 s without a reset) send
            the chip to its ROM download mode when a USB host is attached
            (flash it as usual), else boot SAFE mode: no Muse UI, audio or
            input; Link setup, musegadgets BLE and the BLE update stay up.
            Two crash resets within 10 minutes of each other turn Muse off
            (SAFE mode) until re-enabled (the BLE console's safe.off) or a
            new firmware image boots. On a Wi-Fi + BLE build SAFE mode is
            Bluetooth only: Wi-Fi, the Muse session and Wi-Fi OTA stay off
            (app.c run_safe_bluetooth_only). On a build without musegadgets
            (Wi-Fi Muse, Link only) SAFE keeps Link up: Wi-Fi and the Noise
            session with device.health (MUSE_BOOTGUARD_SAFE_LINK_QUIET
            on Link-only builds). Leaving SAFE: safe.off, the USB console's
            >safe=0, a new image, or a 5 s hold of the talk / boot key. On by
            default only in the BLE gadget builds (musegadgets BLE audio, the
            standalone gadget profiles), whose overlays set it too; other
            builds leave it off and never stay in SAFE. On the
            ESP-Mosaico it sits beside ESP-Iris's own counter (3 crashes
            without 30 s up: Vibe Mode): SAFE comes at the second crash and is
            decided at boot (no restart), so Iris still catches a SAFE boot
            that crashes too.

    config MUSE_BOOTGUARD_BLE_REACHABLE
        bool "The healthy mark needs BLE reachable (and the stay-reachable check)"
        depends on MUSE_BOOTGUARD
        default y if MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE || MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO
        default n
        help
            On builds with musegadgets BLE, BLE is the recovery tier: a boot
            counts healthy only once it advertised or connected (else it's a
            failed boot), and a running gadget that stays out of reach 30 s
            restarts. Off (the builds without musegadgets, whose BLE
            is off or silent once set up): healthy is 30 s of uptime alone,
            and nothing restarts the gadget for being out of reach (a Wi-Fi
            or VM outage is not a crash).

    config MUSE_BOOTGUARD_SAFE_LINK_QUIET
        bool "SAFE on a build without musegadgets: Link's commands off"
        depends on MUSE_BOOTGUARD && !MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO
        default y
        help
            SAFE on a build without musegadgets keeps Link up for recovery.
            Its commands are off: on a
            Link-only build they're all the build does (relay, sensors,
            discover, display, camera, the BLE commands), and on a Wi-Fi
            Muse build several reach Muse's display and session, which SAFE
            never starts. link.register lists only device.health; Wi-Fi,
            the Noise session with device.health (which says safe: true),
            the VM management
            commands, Link setup BLE, the console and the button stay, and
            a Link-only build's status light or screen shows the error
            state.

    config MUSE_GADGET_WDT
        bool "Gadget watchdog over the BLE host, the musegadgets worker, the Muse input task and the Link session"
        depends on ESP_TASK_WDT_EN
        default y if MUSE_GADGET_BLE_STANDALONE || (MUSE_GADGET_BLE_AUDIO && !MUSE_BOARD_ESP_MOSAICO)
        default n
        help
            The gadget watchdog (muse_wdt.h): each of those threads feeds
            the task watchdog from its own loop; one that stops for longer
            than ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S panics the chip (a crash record),
            and the boot guard counts the restart. Needs the task watchdog
            on (ESP_TASK_WDT_EN; the BLE gadget profiles turn it on: 15 s,
            idle tasks watched, a panic). Each user is
            fed at least every 10 s. On a Wi-Fi build the Link session task is watched too,
            in its session loop only: its connect, handshake and reconnect
            waits have their own timeouts and aren't watched.

    config MUSE_HEAP_WATCH
        bool "Bench: log internal-heap lows and each task's heap peaks"
        default n
        select HEAP_USE_HOOKS
        help
            Diagnostics (muse_heapwatch.h): a low-priority task samples the
            free internal heap every 10 ms and logs each new low (512 B
            steps) on heap.watch, to line up with the log around it; with
            ESP-IDF's HEAP_TASK_TRACKING also on, the console's >heap.tasks
            prints each task's internal current and peak use. (Tracking
            costs ~6.7 KB of internal RAM on the StopWatch and panicked in
            its free path from the esp_timer task on IDF v6.0.1:
            use it only for a boot-time look.) Through ESP-IDF's heap hooks
            it also records every internal allocation of 512 B or more
            (time, size, when freed, six return addresses) in a ring the
            console's >heap.allocs prints, for addr2line. Never in a
            release.

endmenu

menu "Task stacks"

    config MUSE_STACKS_IN_PSRAM
        bool "Keep the long-lived app tasks' stacks in PSRAM"
        depends on FREERTOS_TASK_CREATE_ALLOW_EXT_MEM
        depends on SPI_FLASH_AUTO_SUSPEND || (SPIRAM_FETCH_INSTRUCTIONS && SPIRAM_RODATA)
        default n
        help
            muse_stack.h: the Noise session's task (12 KB, and no 12 KB
            internal block held for it from boot), Muse's keeper (6 KB),
            boot (8 KB, until the UI is up) and Wi-Fi scan tasks, the
            musegadgets player (4 KB) and the tunnel's send task (4 KB) put
            their stacks in PSRAM, which hands internal RAM back to Wi-Fi,
            BLE, the codecs' DMA and TLS. Their TCBs stay internal.

            Safe for flash writes, not for flash mapping. With code and
            read-only data in PSRAM (SPIRAM_FETCH_INSTRUCTIONS and
            SPIRAM_RODATA) or flash auto-suspend, ESP-IDF leaves the cache on
            during a flash write (SPI_FLASH_CACHE_NO_DISABLE in
            spi_flash_os_func_app.c), so these tasks may write NVS or flash;
            without them a write turns the cache off and a PSRAM stack
            asserts (the reTerminal Sticky's LVGL note in AGENTS.md), hence
            the dependency. Mapping flash freezes the cache whatever the
            option (esp_mmu_map, and asserts s_task_stack_is_sane_when_cache_frozen
            on a PSRAM stack): esp_partition_mmap, spi_flash_mmap,
            bootloader_mmap, and through them esp_ota_get_state_partition,
            esp_ota_get_boot_partition, esp_ota_mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback,
            esp_ota_mark_app_invalid_rollback_and_reboot,
            esp_ota_set_boot_partition, esp_ota_end, esp_image_verify,
            esp_https_ota and reads of encrypted partitions. So every task
            that can reach one of those stays internal: the musegadgets
            worker (SMP: OTA states, the image check, the healthy mark), the
            Wi-Fi OTA and its verify task, the bug report, Muse's console and
            input tasks (the input task also powers the chip off and waits in
            light sleep), and ESP-IDF's own. tests/test_muse_stack.py checks
            the moved tasks' files call none of them.

endmenu
